# Documentation & Comments Rule

## 1. ARCHITECTURE.md (/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md)
This is the static reference doc — edit sections IN PLACE, never append.

After implementing or significantly modifying a feature, update the 
relevant section(s):
- **Project Overview** — update only if the app's core purpose changes
- **Folder Structure** — update if new directories are added or 
  reorganized
- **Features** — add a new ### [Feature Name] block for new features, 
  or update an existing block if that feature was modified. Format:
  - **What it does:** short paragraph
  - **Files involved:** bullet list of paths
  - **Key decisions:** any non-obvious logic or tradeoffs
- **Data Flow** — update if the feature changes how data moves between 
  UI, backend, or database
- **Decisions Log** — add a new row to the table for any meaningful 
  architectural or design decision (why X over Y), with reasoning and 
  date/context

Do not rewrite unrelated sections. Do not change the header structure.

## 2. CHANGELOG.md (/docs/CHANGELOG.md)
This is the living history — APPEND ONLY, never edit past entries, 
newest entry on top.

After implementing or significantly modifying a feature, add a new entry:
[YYYY-MM-DD] [Feature name]

Summary: what was built
Files: created/modified paths
Reasoning: why it was built this way

One entry per feature/task, not per commit or per file.

## 3. Code Comments
When writing or modifying code, add comments that explain the logic — 
not line-by-line, enough that a new engineer can skim and understand 
intent.

- Short comment block at the top of each new file/module explaining 
  its purpose and where it fits in the architecture
- Comment above each function/class explaining what it does and why 
  it exists (not just restating its name)
- Comment non-obvious logic: tricky conditionals, workarounds, 
  algorithm/approach choices
- Do NOT comment self-explanatory code (e.g. no "// increment counter" 
  above i++)
- Roughly one comment block per function or logical section — not per 
  statement

## 4. nucleus_flutter_app_fe530d03.plan.md (.cursor/plans/nucleus_flutter_app_fe530d03.plan.md)
Only modify the existing plan when the user explicitly updates, modifies, or adds to the plan, or explicitly asks to make changes or additions to it. Do not create a new plan file unless the user explicitly requests one.

## Workflow
Steps 1-3 happen as part of the same task that implements the feature or fixes a bug — not as a separate follow-up. Before ending a coding task, check: did 
this change touch a feature (update ARCHITECTURE.md + CHANGELOG.md)? 
Does the new/changed code have appropriate comments per the rules above (if no add appropriate comments)?